\title{Example constructive and non-constructive proofs}
\date{2026-03-02T17:44:54Z}
\tag{discrete}
\tag{draft}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{As an example, consider the proposition "for every natural number, there is a larger number which is not a square".  We might encode this in formal propositional logic as follows:

\transclude{008V}

where as usual we define #{\Square(m)} by

\transclude{008W}

This proposition is true, and we will see two different ways we might prove it.  In either case, we will first need the following lemma.}

\transclude{008X}

\proof{
  Let #{n} be an arbitrary natural number; we must show #{\ext{m}{\N} (m > n) \land (\neg \Square(n))}, which we will do by proving the logically equivalent proposition ##{\neg \neg (\ext{n}{\N} (n > p) \land (\neg \Prime(n))),} which is in turn equivalent to ##{\neg (\all{n}{\N} (n > p) \to \Prime(n))} (see [[0047]] and [[0049]] for a refresher if you're not sure why these are equivalent).  So [suppose](005N) #{\all{n}{\N} (n > p) \to \Prime(n)}; we will prove #{\False}.
  \subproof{
    XXX
  }
}

\p{This proof (besides being unnecessarily complicated) is unsatisfying in exactly the way [discussed previously](007V): it establishes that there \em{must exist} a number bigger than #{p} which is not prime, but it doesn't really tell us how to find one!  Here is a different proof:}

\proof{
  Let #{p} be an arbitrary prime, and consider the number #{2p}.  Clearly #{2p > p}, and also #{2p} is not prime, since it is divisible by #{2}.
}

\p{This proof directly tells us how to calculate, XXX}

